sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch11-20> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because a septum is first laid down as an elastic membrane under uniform pressure, it takes the shape of a soap film with the same boundary, a surface of minimal area: spherical in a straight cone with a perpendicular attachment, ellipsoidal with an oblique attachment, and saddle-shaped where the line of attachment is sinuous.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Cephalopod septa take the form of minimal surfaces like soap films";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XI, “The Logarithmic Spiral”; topic “Septa of chambered shells”. Scope: septa of nautiloid, orthocone and ammonoid shells"
}
